Sigh...  Probabilities seem to be difficult for most people...

If I need ten boulders to cover an area of a moat, what is the 
probability that at least one will sink?  It is 1-0.9^10 = 0.65.

If I need two boulders to cover a small moat of only two squares, what 
is the probability that one will sink?  1-0.9^2 = 0.19.

Are you going to tell me that 0.19 is the same as 0.65?

> No matter how deep, wide, big, large, expansive, or oceanic an area of
> water in NetHack might be, the chance of a boulder sinking will *always*
> be 10%. Per boulder, on any case, even on average. There's also no
> difference between moats and pools, IIRC.

I was not *at any time* talking about the probability of an *elementary* 
event, i.e. the sinking of one boulder in one try.  That was settled at 
the moment the figure 0.1 was given.  I was, and this should be clear 
from the phrase that I used about "filling a moat" (also I sent a second 
post, just to make it hundred percent clear), talking about the 
probability of getting a sinking boulder when you try to fill a large 
moat in comparison with a small one.  (Whether you call it a moat or a 
pool, I don't care.)

The fact that this probability is larger in large moats than in small 
moats *might* be considered an effort to simulate, in a very crude way 
what happens in real life.  Probably it is not so, since the DevTeam for 
one time did *not* think of everything.  Nevertheless, I considered it a 
nice speculation.  And of course, the remark by Dylan that nothing else 
was intended than to implement Murphy's law, is much less convincing. 
For Murphy's law would have it that the boulders sink with 90% 
probability rather than 10% :-).
